\description{
Run the machine learning based adaptive EWMA chart for process dispersion
on subgroup data. Each subgroup variance is transformed as in Castagliola
(2005), a pre-fitted model predicts the smoothing weight \code{theta}
(restricted to [0, 1]) and the chart statistic is updated as
\code{AEWMA_t = theta_t * D_t + (1 - theta_t) * AEWMA_(t-1)}. The first
subgroup with \verb{|AEWMA_t| > h} is the first signal.
}
\details{
The four functions share all arguments. \code{mlaewma_neural()} offers \code{"nn"},
\code{"qrnn"} and \code{"mcqrnn"}; \code{mlaewma_svm()} offers \code{"svr"}, \code{"svqr"} and
\code{"rsvqr"}; \code{mlaewma_tree()} offers \code{"dt"} and \code{"gb"}; \code{mlaewma_dqr()} has the
single model \code{"dqr"}, whose smoothing weight also depends on the two
previous values of \code{D}. With \code{method = "all"} every model of the family is
run and compared (for the one-model DQR family \code{"all"} is the same as
\code{"dqr"}). The \code{method} is not case sensitive. The models, the
transformation constants and the control limits are calibrated for
subgroups of size 5 only.

\code{first_signal} is the number of the first subgroup that signals, counted
from the start of \code{data}; it is \code{NA} if the chart never signals.

\strong{Choosing \code{sigma0}.} \code{sigma0} must be the standard deviation of the
process while it is in control, estimated from a clean reference sample.
If it is left \code{NULL}, the standard deviation of all supplied observations is
used, which is inflated when \code{data} already contains an out-of-control
stretch and can hide the shift.

\strong{Limitations of the models.} The charts are calibrated for an in-control
ARL of about 370 (within roughly 2 percent; the bisection tolerance is
+/- 5). Their ability to detect changes differs a lot between models and
is not guaranteed:
\itemize{
\item \code{"qrnn"} and \code{"mcqrnn"} assign almost no weight to large positive \code{D};
their average run length for a moderate variance increase (variance
multiplier 1.5) is longer than the in-control one. They are not suitable
for monitoring variance increases.
\item \code{"nn"}, \code{"dt"}, \code{"gb"} and \code{"dqr"} detect variance increases but
practically not decreases. \code{"svr"}, \code{"svqr"} and \code{"rsvqr"} react to both
directions.
\item The models were trained on values of the transformed statistic between 0
and 1.9, but about half of the in-control values of \code{D} are negative, so
the predicted weights are partly extrapolated.
}

See the vignette for simulated comparisons.

Simulation (needed when \code{find_h = TRUE} or \code{shifts} is given) is controlled
by \code{n_streams} and \code{n_cores}; see \code{\link[=calculate_arl]{calculate_arl()}}. The results depend on
\code{seed}, \code{n_streams} and the number of simulations, not on \code{n_cores}.
}
